  "preamble": "Implosivization of a voiceless labial stop: plain [p] acquires glottal closure and voicing and becomes the implosive (or preglottalized) [ɓ]. In Vietnamese, after Old Vietnamese lost the voicing contrast on stops (voiced *b merging with *p and leaving only a low-register tone), the stops were re-implosivized, p > ɓ (Ferlus 1992), so Proto-Vietic *p and *b both surface as Vietnamese b [ʔɓ]; the inherited Proto-Vietic implosive *ɓ had meanwhile become m. Khmer shows the same outcome earlier, before Old Khmer: Proto-Mon-Khmer *p became [ɓ] in these words. Old Khmer spelling wrote both [p] and [ɓ] as p, so Old Khmer ⟨pi⟩ 'three' already stood for [ɓiː] (Wikipedia, Old Khmer, after Sidwell 2014). Vietnamese [ɓ] has strong preglottalization and is sometimes released as [ʔb].",
